const additions:Record<string,ArticleSection[]>={
  'philippa-foot':[
    section('justice-benevolence-life','Justice, benevolence, abortion, and human life',[
      'Foot’s essays connect virtue with more than the agent’s private flourishing. Justice concerns what people may claim from one another and restrains the temptation to secure one’s own good through injury or unfairness. Benevolence or charity makes another person’s need practically salient. These virtues can pull in different directions: benevolence favors rescue and relief, while justice may forbid using an unwilling person as material for a beneficial outcome. This tension helps explain why Foot did not reduce morality to maximizing welfare or to cultivating an admirable personality.',
      'In “Euthanasia,” Foot distinguishes questions that are often collapsed: whether an act is contrary to justice, whether it accords with charity, and whether death can sometimes be a benefit rather than a harm. She analyzes active and passive euthanasia without assuming that every killing is worse than every allowing to die. The essay’s conclusions remain disputed, but its method is characteristic: describe the good and harm at stake, ask what rights constrain action, and then ask what benevolence requires. Neither a slogan about life’s sanctity nor a simple appeal to consequences settles the case.',
      'Her abortion paper is similarly narrower and more exploratory than a comprehensive theory of pregnancy. It begins from uncertainty about fetal moral status and examines whether double effect explains judgments about abortion and other conflicts. The trolley comparison emerges inside that inquiry. Foot later criticized Thomson’s violinist strategy because she thought abortion could involve bringing about death rather than merely declining life support; Thomson’s paper, in turn, reorganized the issue around bodily use and rights. Their disagreement concerns the structure of action, not two versions of one “trolley” doctrine.'
    ],['ethics','virtue-ethics'],['judith-thomson','anscombe'],['Euthanasia','The Problem of Abortion and the Doctrine of the Double Effect']),
    section('development-reasons','A changing account of reasons and rationality',[
      'Foot’s career contains a real change of position. Her early naturalism opposed noncognitivism by arguing that evaluations of virtue and vice answer to facts about harm, benefit, and the human practices in which action becomes intelligible. Yet she came to doubt that calling an act immoral by itself showed every agent that the act frustrated an end they already had. In “Morality as a System of Hypothetical Imperatives” she therefore denied that the special authority often attached to the moral “ought” had been established simply by contrasting it with hypothetical imperatives.',
      'The mature Natural Goodness does not merely repeat that middle-period position. Foot returns to the rational authority of morality through a different account of practical reason. Human willing can be evaluated against natural-historical facts about human beings: we depend on one another, use language, make promises, pursue goods across time, and act for reasons. Justice and benevolence are not optional techniques for satisfying whatever desires happen to occur; defects of will can be defects in responding to the reasons characteristic of a good human life.',
      'Whether the mature argument succeeds remains open. Critics ask how a description of the human life form selects genuine normative standards without idealizing conformity, excluding disability, or importing inherited hierarchy. They also ask whether facts about flourishing can explain why a person has decisive reason to be just when injustice serves that person’s interests. Defenders reply that life-form judgment is neither statistical normality nor evolutionary fitness and that practical rationality is itself evaluative. This dispute is central to Foot’s later philosophy.'
    ],['ethics','virtue-ethics'],['aristotle','anscombe'],['Moral Beliefs','Morality as a System of Hypothetical Imperatives','Natural Goodness']),
  ],
  'judith-thomson':[
    section('samaritans-consent-property','Good Samaritans, consent, responsibility, and property',[
      'A Defense of Abortion distinguishes justice from generosity through the figures of the Good Samaritan and the Minimally Decent Samaritan. Someone may lack a right to another person’s bodily support even when refusing modest aid would be indecent. Thomson does not conclude that whatever is within one’s rights is admirable, nor that positive duties never exist. She asks which assistance may be legally or morally demanded and which exceeds an enforceable claim. That separation between permission, decency, and generosity keeps the paper from becoming a simple assertion that bodily control always defeats every competing consideration.',
      'Consent and responsibility complicate the violinist analogy. Thomson considers cases in which people take precautions, voluntarily accept a risk, or participate in an activity from which dependency results. Her “people-seeds” example argues that opening a window despite installing protective screens does not automatically grant an intruder a right to remain. Critics answer that pregnancy, parenthood, and causal responsibility are unlike a drifting seed or kidnapping. One analogy cannot decide the issue: responsibility must be shown to generate a particular claim to bodily use rather than merely invoked as a word.',
      'Property also enters Thomson’s larger rights theory in a qualified way. A right to exclude someone from a house, coat, or resource depends on how the entitlement arose; possession alone does not settle legitimacy. The Realm of Rights roots ordinary property claims in a legitimate legal order and in transactions that generate duties. Bodily claims in the abortion essay therefore should not be translated without argument into an absolute doctrine of self-ownership. Thomson’s work concerns structured interpersonal claims, not rights that never conflict.'
    ],['ethics','political-philosophy'],['judith-thomson'],['A Defense of Abortion','The Realm of Rights']),
    section('revisions-over-decades','Revising cases rather than freezing intuitions',[
      'Thomson’s trolley work changed. Foot’s 1967 driver diverts an existing threat; Thomson’s 1976 essay named the trolley problem and examined killing and letting die; the 1985 paper introduced the now-standard Bystander at the Switch and compared it with Footbridge. That sequence already shows that “the trolley problem” is not one immutable scenario. Each alteration changes who controls the threat, whether the victim is on the path, and whether the victim’s presence is needed as a means.',
      'In “Turning the Trolley” (2008), Thomson reconsidered her famous judgment and argued that a bystander may not turn the trolley onto the one. Her revised reasoning asks why the bystander may impose death on the one to spare the five when none has a stronger claim to the bystander’s intervention. This late reversal demonstrates that the cases were not devices for recording a settled intuition but parts of an argument Thomson remained willing to revise.',
      'The reversal does not erase the earlier literature or yield a universal prohibition on redirection. It changes which proposed explanations need defense and highlights differences among a driver responsible for a threat, an uninvolved bystander, and a person who uses another as a tool. Readers should attach dates and agents to each case. “Thomson’s trolley view” otherwise becomes a textbook composite that no single essay, and not even Thomson across the decades, straightforwardly endorses.'
    ],['ethics','analytic-philosophy'],['philippa-foot'],['Killing, Letting Die, and the Trolley Problem','The Trolley Problem','Turning the Trolley']),
  ],
  'thomas-nagel':[
    section('war-massacre','War, massacre, and moral constraint',[
      'In “War and Massacre,” written against the background of the Vietnam War, Nagel argues that the conduct of war cannot be judged only by aggregate results. An absolutist restriction addresses an agent directly: some ways of treating another person are forbidden even when breaking the restriction might avert greater harm. The victim is not merely one item in a calculation but someone toward whom the agent acts. Nagel’s account is deontological without pretending that every prohibition is easy to formulate or that catastrophe exerts no moral pressure.',
      'The essay distinguishes aiming at a person as the object of hostile or coercive action from causing harm as a side effect, but Nagel does not offer a mechanical version of double effect. He examines the character of the action, the relation between agent and victim, and whether the victim is treated as a human being whose circumstances matter. Rules governing combatants, civilians, weapons, and surrender attempt to express this interpersonal limit. War makes those constraints difficult to honor; it does not automatically suspend them.',
      'Critics ask whether absolutism can guide action when every option violates a serious constraint, whether intention is too manipulable, and whether Nagel’s framework attends enough to institutions that create war. Those objections matter. Still, the essay belongs at the center of his ethics because it shows the personal and impersonal standpoints under extreme pressure: public policy seeks overall survival or victory, while agency remains answerable for what one person deliberately does to another.'
    ],['ethics','political-philosophy','deontology'],['thomas-nagel'],['War and Massacre','Mortal Questions']),
    section('political-legitimacy-global-justice','Political legitimacy, equality, and global justice',[
      'Equality and Partiality asks how a legitimate political system can integrate personal and impersonal standpoints within citizens rather than demand saintly impersonality. Its argument moves through unanimity, the moral division of labor, egalitarianism, motivation, rights, toleration, and the limits of justice. Institutions may shoulder impartial tasks that individuals cannot continuously perform while leaving protected options for personal commitments. Coercive rules must be justifiable to people who retain their own lives, not only to ideal spectators.',
      'Nagel’s later “Problem of Global Justice” distinguishes humanitarian morality from demanding principles of socioeconomic justice. He argues that the latter arise through a sovereign state’s coercive structure and citizens’ joint authorship of law, so they do not yet apply globally in the same form. Cosmopolitan critics reject this state-centered boundary, noting that global institutions, markets, borders, and historical power also coerce and shape life chances. The controversy remains open: Nagel offers a political conception of justice, not a conclusion that severe global inequality is morally acceptable.'
    ],['political-philosophy','ethics'],['rawls'],['Equality and Partiality','The Problem of Global Justice']),
    section('mind-cosmos','Mind and Cosmos: a disputed later naturalism',[
      'Mind and Cosmos extends Nagel’s antireductionism from conscious organisms to the natural history that produced them. He argues that if consciousness, rational cognition, and objective value are real and not reducible to physical description, then a complete conception of nature must explain their possibility rather than treat them as accidental appearances. The book rejects theism and intelligent design as explanations, but it also rejects materialist neo-Darwinism as complete. Nagel canvasses psychophysical laws, emergence, and natural teleology as possibilities rather than presenting a finished scientific theory.',
      'The teleological proposal is explicitly speculative: natural tendencies toward life, mind, and value might belong to nature without being the intentions of a designer. Nagel does not deny common descent, mutation, selection, molecular biology, or the practical success of physical science. He argues that those resources, interpreted through a materialist metaphysics, do not yet explain why conscious and reasoning beings were possible. This is far stronger than the bat essay’s narrower challenge and should not be projected backward as though the 1974 paper already contained the later cosmology.',
      'The book drew severe criticism. Reviewers argued that Nagel moved too quickly from gaps in present explanation to limits on materialist evolutionary accounts, underestimated work on cognition and value, and offered no testable teleological mechanism. Sympathetic readers saw continuity with his long resistance to reducing first-person and normative phenomena. A responsible account should neither dismiss the book as anti-science nor present its alternatives as established. It is a contested late proposal whose evidential burden remains unmet in the eyes of many philosophers and scientists.'
    ],['philosophy-of-mind','metaphysics','philosophy-of-science'],['thomas-nagel'],['Mind and Cosmos','What Is It Like to Be a Bat?']),
  ],
  'derek-parfit':[
    section('relation-r-branching','Relation R, branching, and what matters in survival',[
      'Parfit calls the relevant psychological relation “Relation R”: psychological connectedness and continuity with the right kind of cause. Connectedness consists in direct links such as memories, intentions, and continuing desires; continuity consists in overlapping chains of strong connectedness. Ordinary identity normally travels with these relations, but Parfit argues that identity is not the further fact that makes survival matter. What matters can come in degrees because psychological relations can weaken gradually even though numerical identity cannot be partial.',
      'Branching cases expose the difference. If a person’s psychology were continued in two future people, neither could be numerically identical with the original because one person cannot be identical with two distinct people. Yet both successors could stand in Relation R to the earlier person. Parfit concludes that the failure of identity need not be equivalent to ordinary death. Teletransportation and division are argumentative probes: they isolate features of survival and prudential concern rather than predictions about actual technology.',
      'The conclusion is reductionist but not eliminativist. Persons exist, act, suffer, and bear responsibilities; facts about them consist in physical and psychological facts without an additional indivisible ego. Critics dispute whether continuity can preserve first-person anticipation, responsibility, embodiment, or the unity of a life. Parfit’s narrower claim is that no separately existing Cartesian subject is required to explain identity and that our practical concerns may rationally track Relation R instead.'
    ],['ethics','philosophy-of-mind','metaphysics'],['derek-parfit'],['Reasons and Persons']),
    section('self-defeat-triple-theory','Self-defeating moral theories and the later Triple Theory',[
      'Reasons and Persons begins with theories that can be individually or collectively self-defeating. A theory may instruct each person to act in ways that predictably frustrate the aims the theory gives them; common-sense morality, self-interest theory, and consequentialism face different versions of this pressure. Parfit uses these cases to ask whether rationality is essentially self-interested and whether moral principles must be assessed partly by what would happen if people successfully followed them.',
      'On What Matters shifts from identity and future people to reasons and possible convergence among moral theories. Parfit’s Triple Theory claims, in a carefully revised form, that the best versions of Kantian contractualism, Scanlonian contractualism, and rule consequentialism may select the same acts: those permitted by principles whose universal acceptance would make things go best, when no one could reasonably reject those principles and everyone could rationally will them. This is a proposed convergence after substantial modification, not proof that the traditions have always said the same thing.',
      'The project remains contested. Critics dispute Parfit’s non-naturalist account of objective normative truths, the demandingness and aggregation built into convergence, and whether contractualism or Kantian morality loses its distinctive basis when reconstructed around optimific principles. Parfit revised formulations and answered critics across three volumes. The later project should therefore be read as an ongoing argument about reasons and moral agreement, not as the settled endpoint of Reasons and Persons.'
    ],['ethics','deontology','consequentialism'],['kant','rawls'],['Reasons and Persons','On What Matters']),
  ],
  'martha-nussbaum':[
    section('capabilities-functionings-sen','Capabilities, functionings, and the relation to Sen',[
      'A functioning is an achieved being or doing—being nourished, moving freely, taking part in political life—while a capability is a real opportunity to achieve combinations of functionings. Nussbaum normally makes capabilities the political target because adults should have space to choose whether to exercise many opportunities. The distinction prevents the approach from simply requiring everyone to live one officially favored life, although children, health, and other cases complicate the priority of opportunity over achieved functioning.',
      'Nussbaum and Amartya Sen developed related but distinct versions of the capability approach. Sen emphasizes comparative evaluation, public reasoning, and resistance to a single fixed list; Nussbaum proposes a list of central capabilities and a threshold below which a society fails to secure human dignity. Her list is meant as abstract, revisable, and open to local specification within political liberalism, not as an exhaustive ranking of every good life. Even so, critics ask who sets the list and how revision works under unequal power.',
      'Implementation raises further disputes. Capabilities can be difficult to measure, institutions must decide how to distribute resources among them, and cultural variation can make universal language look paternalistic. Nussbaum replies that entrenched preferences may themselves reflect deprivation and that a cross-cultural political minimum can protect plural choices. The exchange concerns how universal dignity and democratic self-government should constrain one another, not a choice between respecting culture and caring about deprivation.'
    ],['political-philosophy','ethics','feminist-philosophy'],['sen','rawls'],['Women and Human Development','Creating Capabilities']),
    section('frontiers-emotion-humanities','Disability, animals, global justice, emotion, and the humanities',[
      'Frontiers of Justice tests social-contract theories against people with cognitive and physical disabilities, inequalities among nations, and human treatment of nonhuman animals. Nussbaum argues that cooperation for mutual advantage cannot be the sole basis of justice because some beings who deserve justice cannot participate on equal contractual terms. Species membership, dependency, care, and forms of flourishing require institutions to secure capabilities beyond the standard image of independent citizens bargaining as equals.',
      'Her account of emotion is equally central. In Upheavals of Thought, emotions are intelligent appraisals of things outside our control that matter to our flourishing; they are not mere bodily surges opposed to reason. The Fragility of Goodness uses Greek tragedy and philosophy to explore how luck and attachment expose a good life to loss. Later political work asks how compassion, fear, disgust, grief, and love can support or deform public culture. Critics challenge the cognitive theory and worry that state-sponsored emotion can become manipulative.',
      'Literature and the humanities matter because narrative can train attention to lives unlike one’s own and reveal particulars that general rules miss. Nussbaum’s legal advocacy and public commentary draw on this philosophy but should not be confused with it: a brief, judicial argument, or newspaper intervention has a different evidential and argumentative setting from a philosophical book. Across these domains, her recurring question is how vulnerable embodied beings can receive material support, recognition, education, and political standing consistent with equal dignity.'
    ],['political-philosophy','ethics','aesthetics'],['aristotle','rawls'],['The Fragility of Goodness','Upheavals of Thought','Frontiers of Justice']),
  ],
  'judith-butler':[
    section('ethical-relation-opacity','Ethical relation, opacity, and responsibility',[
      'Giving an Account of Oneself moves from the formation of subjects to the ethics of address. Because social norms, language, and relations precede us, no person can give a fully transparent account of becoming who they are. This opacity does not cancel responsibility. It can make judgment less self-righteous and orient responsibility toward the scene in which one answers another person whose life is also not completely knowable.',
      'Butler’s ethics draws on recognition while criticizing demands that recognition force everyone into established categories. An account is given to someone and shaped by norms that decide which stories count as coherent. Ethical relation therefore includes both accountability for conduct and criticism of the terms under which subjects become recognizable. Vulnerability is not a private weakness to overcome but a shared, unequally distributed condition of embodied dependency.',
      'This phase is continuous with, but not identical to, Gender Trouble. The early genealogy of gender categories does not already supply the later account of ethical address, grief, war, assembly, and nonviolence. Reading the corpus chronologically shows a widening field: the reiteration of norms remains important, while questions of bodily need, dispossession, obligation, and political action receive increasingly direct treatment.'
    ],['ethics','feminist-philosophy','continental-philosophy'],['levinas','foucault'],['Giving an Account of Oneself','Precarious Life']),
    section('war-assembly-nonviolence','Grievability, assembly, Jewish ethics, and nonviolence',[
      'Precarious Life and Frames of War ask how public frames make some deaths apprehensible and grievable while rendering others unreal or inevitable. Media conventions, state discourse, borders, and war organize perception before an explicit judgment occurs. Butler’s discussions of Jewish ethical traditions and Israel/Palestine use diaspora, cohabitation, and the prohibition on dispossession to criticize nationalism and state violence. These arguments are intensely disputed and should be separated from polemical summaries of Butler’s public positions.',
      'Notes Toward a Performative Theory of Assembly treats gathering bodies as political claims. An assembly does not merely express a prior popular will; bodies occupying and moving through space can enact demands for livability, expose dependency on infrastructure, and contest who counts as “the people.” This extends performativity from gendered norms to collective action without claiming that speech or gesture alone changes material conditions. Food, shelter, transport, care, and policing shape who can appear.',
      'The Force of Nonviolence argues for an aggressive, collective nonviolence grounded in interdependency and the equal grievability of lives. Butler rejects both passive moral purity and the fantasy of a self-contained individual choosing violence only in self-defense. Critics question definitions of violence, applications to particular conflicts, and whether nonviolence can answer acute threats. The proposal is a later ethical-political argument, not a hidden premise of every earlier work or a verdict that dissolves hard cases.'
    ],['ethics','political-philosophy','feminist-philosophy'],['arendt','levinas'],['Precarious Life','Frames of War','Notes Toward a Performative Theory of Assembly','The Force of Nonviolence']),
  ],
  'angela-davis':[
    section('constructive-abolition','The prison-industrial complex and constructive abolition',[
      'Davis describes the prison-industrial complex as a changing alignment of punishment, racial hierarchy, political incentives, labor, corporate interests, and public habits that makes imprisonment appear to solve social problems. The phrase does not mean that private prison companies alone cause mass incarceration. Public institutions, sentencing, policing, deindustrialization, media, and the withdrawal of social provision can all help produce carceral expansion. Abolition therefore requires more than changing ownership or closing one building.',
      'Abolition Democracy presents abolition as both negative and constructive. The destruction of slavery was incomplete without institutions that could make freedom substantive; likewise, reducing prisons must be joined to education, housing, health care, democratic participation, conflict resolution, and responses to violence that do not reproduce abandonment. Davis does not provide one finished blueprint. She frames abolition as an experimental political process that transforms the conditions under which harm occurs and expands collective capacity to address it.',
      'Critics press abolitionists about severe interpersonal violence, transition, accountability, and institutional feasibility. Davis’s reply is not that harm disappears or that every reform is useless. She asks whether reforms reduce the reach and legitimacy of incarceration while building noncarceral capacities, or instead modernize and entrench the system. That criterion is influential but contested, and later abolitionist movements develop strategies that should not all be attributed directly to Davis.'
    ],['political-philosophy','feminist-philosophy','marxism'],['angela-davis','foucault'],['Are Prisons Obsolete?','Abolition Democracy']),
    section('communism-internationalism-genres','Communism, internationalism, and different kinds of writing',[
      'Davis’s Marxism and long Communist affiliations shape her analysis of racial capitalism, labor, imperialism, political prisoners, and collective struggle. They are neither incidental biography nor a complete explanation of every position. Women, Race & Class reconstructs conflicts among abolition, suffrage, labor, and racism; Blues Legacies and Black Feminism reads cultural form and sexual expression; Freedom Is a Constant Struggle gathers speeches and interviews linking movements across places. These genres make different kinds of claims and should not be treated as interchangeable treatises.',
      'Internationalism is a method of comparison and solidarity, not a claim that distinct histories are identical. Davis connects struggles against apartheid, occupation, policing, incarceration, and gendered violence while emphasizing movements rather than heroic individuals. Critics dispute particular alliances, analyses of state violence, and the political history of communist organizations. A responsible account records these disputes without making association substitute for examination of arguments.',
      'Her prosecution and 1972 acquittal affected her public life, but the facts require care: a jury acquitted Davis of all charges after a highly publicized trial. Her philosophical education included study in Frankfurt and graduate work at the University of California, San Diego with Herbert Marcuse; it should not be inflated into a completed Humboldt doctorate. Biography matters here because repression, academic freedom, organizing, and international campaigns informed her later thinking, not because the trial supplies the organizing principle of abolitionist philosophy.'
    ],['political-philosophy','feminist-philosophy','marxism'],['marx','angela-davis'],['Women, Race & Class','Blues Legacies and Black Feminism','Freedom Is a Constant Struggle','Angela Davis: An Autobiography']),
  ],
  'bell-hooks':[
    section('terminology-strategy','Interlocking domination, intersectionality, and feminist strategy',[
      'hooks argues that feminism should oppose sexist oppression rather than seek equal access for a privileged group of women within existing hierarchies. From Margin to Center criticizes movements that universalized the experience of white, middle-class women and treated race and class as secondary additions. The margin can disclose relations hidden from the center, but hooks does not romanticize deprivation: the political aim is a mass movement able to transform domination rather than reserve theory for academic specialists.',
      'Her analyses of race, class, gender, and imperialism substantively overlap later intersectional work, but the histories and vocabularies should remain distinct. Kimberlé Crenshaw introduced “intersectionality” in a specific legal-critical argument in 1989; hooks had already analyzed interlocking systems without making that later term her signature concept. Calling hooks an intersectional thinker can be a useful comparison if it does not erase chronology, differences of method, or the concepts she actually used.',
      'This strategic account also brings men, family, labor, sexuality, and religion into feminist criticism. hooks argues that men are shaped and wounded by patriarchy while still receiving and exercising patriarchal power. She rejects the conclusion that liberation requires hostility toward men or that family and spiritual practice are inherently emancipatory. Critics dispute moments of generalization and essentialism, but the corpus repeatedly revises familiar sites of intimacy as possible locations of both domination and change.'
    ],['feminist-philosophy','political-philosophy','ethics'],['bell-hooks','angela-davis'],['Ain’t I a Woman','Feminist Theory: From Margin to Center','The Will to Change']),
    section('love-community-pedagogy','Love, beloved community, and engaged pedagogy',[
      'All About Love defines love through a combination of care, affection, recognition, respect, commitment, trust, knowledge, responsibility, and honest communication. Love is therefore an ethical practice rather than a feeling that excuses domination. hooks connects failures of love to patriarchal control, consumer culture, childhood violence, and emotional denial. The book’s popular form does not make it apolitical, though its broad claims invite criticism about psychology, religion, and how intimate change relates to institutions.',
      'The beloved community names a horizon in which people build relationships across difference without denying conflict, accountability, or histories of domination. It joins love to collective practice and helps explain why hooks writes about classrooms, households, media, masculinity, and movement culture in the same corpus. Spiritual language is one resource in this vision, not a demand for doctrinal agreement. Critics ask whether the ideal gives enough guidance for coercive institutions and entrenched material inequality.',
      'Engaged pedagogy translates these commitments into education. Drawing from Paulo Freire while criticizing sexism and attending to the whole person, hooks treats students as participants in knowledge rather than passive containers. Voice and experience matter, but neither is beyond question; teachers retain responsibilities for preparation, structure, and the conditions of dialogue. The model can be difficult under unequal classrooms and precarious institutions. Its enduring force lies in joining critical consciousness with the practice, risk, and care of learning together.'
    ],['feminist-philosophy','ethics','political-philosophy'],['bell-hooks','angela-davis'],['Teaching to Transgress','All About Love','The Will to Change']),
  ],
};

const replaceParagraph=(sections:ArticleSection[],sectionId:string,index:number,text:string):ArticleSection[]=>sections.map((item)=>item.id!==sectionId?item:{...item,paragraphs:item.paragraphs.map((paragraph,paragraphIndex)=>paragraphIndex===index?text:paragraph)});

const revisedArticles=(record:Philosopher):ArticleSection[]=>{
  let current=[...(record.articleSections??[])];
  if(record.id==='philippa-foot')current=replaceParagraph(current,'trolley',0,'Foot introduced the runaway-trolley comparison in her 1967 paper on abortion and the doctrine of double effect. Her driver can steer a trolley away from five people and toward one. The comparison belongs to an argument about what an agent does versus allows and about negative duties not to injure versus positive duties to provide aid. Foot did not present a timeless “switch case,” a survey of intuition, or the later bystander-and-footbridge family associated with Thomson. The original setting matters because she was testing whether intention alone explains differences among killing, redirecting a threat, and withholding assistance.');
  if(record.id==='judith-thomson')current=replaceParagraph(current,'rights',1,'The Realm of Rights begins by asking what it is to have a right before cataloguing which rights people possess. Thomson analyzes claims, privileges, powers, duties, enforcement, and the tradeoffs that can relieve an agent of an otherwise applicable requirement. She distinguishes rights people have as human beings—centrally claims against being killed or harmed—from rights generated by promises, consent, law, and other transactions. Needs do not automatically generate claims that others meet them, and property rights depend on a legitimate legal system rather than simple moral self-ownership. This systematic structure is the background against which her cases should be read.');
  if(record.id==='thomas-nagel')current=current.filter(({id})=>id!=='controversies');
  const extra=additions[record.id]??[];
  const readingIndex=current.findIndex(({id})=>id==='reading-strategy');
  return readingIndex<0?[...current,...extra]:[...current.slice(0,readingIndex),...extra,...current.slice(readingIndex)];
};

